class.description:
Knows every [Viewport] (including [SubViewport]s and [Window]s) that is inside the [SceneTree], so settings and tools don't have to search the tree for them. It also finds the node behind a viewport [RID] or a window ID.
The graphics settings servers use it for [constant SettingsServer.VIEWPORT_TARGET_ALL_VIEWPORTS], for example [member RenderQualitySettingsServer.viewport_target].
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
func _ready():
    for window in ViewportServer.get_windows():
        print(window.title, " has window ID ", window.get_window_id())
    ViewportServer.viewport_added.connect(_on_viewport_added)

func _on_viewport_added(viewport):
    viewport.msaa_3d = Viewport.MSAA_4X
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
public override void _Ready()
{
    foreach (Window window in ViewportServer.GetWindows())
    {
        GD.Print(window.Title, " has window ID ", window.GetWindowId());
    }
    ViewportServer.ViewportAdded += OnViewportAdded;
}

private void OnViewportAdded(Viewport viewport)
{
    viewport.Msaa3D = Viewport.Msaa.Msaa4X;
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
The registry follows [signal SceneTree.node_added] and [signal SceneTree.node_removed]. It starts at the first frame, or earlier at the first call of one of its methods, and then lists every viewport that is already in the tree. [signal viewport_added] is only emitted for viewports that enter the tree after that, so call [method get_viewports] for the ones that are there already.
[b]Note:[/b] Viewports that are not inside the tree are not listed. In the editor, the registry also lists the editor's own viewports.
[b]Note:[/b] Use this server from the main thread only, like the scene tree.
